		<div class="blog-separator">&nbsp;</div>
	
		<div class="blog-content">
				<div class="paragraph"><span><span style="color:rgb(102, 102, 102)">Reflections on what I learned attending the 2025 UN Global Goals Week.</span></span><br /><span></span></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/og-about-us-social-global-sdgs_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Photo courtesy of Ricoh USA, Global SDG Action Month.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span><span>For one week, New York City transitions from the world&rsquo;s crossroads into a stage for our collective ambition. Roads are blocked off, protests exist on every street corner, and your bus may be delayed, like it was for me. But that is for very good reason. Each September, New York City transforms into the capital of the world. Diplomats, CEOs, advocates, students, and observers watch on for our world's most prominent week. Climate Week, the UN Global Goals Week, and the United Nations General Assembly unfold side by side, elevating the city into the epicenter of global impact.</span></span><span><span><br /><br />Global Goals Week is the chance to shed light on the United Nations&rsquo; </span><a href="https://sdgs.un.org/goals"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">Sustainable Development Goals</span></a><span>, also known as SDGs. They were enacted in 2015 as a universal call to action that set 17 ambitious goals to guide nations toward a fairer and more sustainable world by 2030. These goals represent our future, and what that future may look like depends on the willingness of leaders across the globe to act.</span></span><span><span><br /><br />For 2025, this was my first time attending, primarily for Global Goals Week, now serving as a UNA-USA Global Goals Ambassador for </span><a href="https://sdgs.un.org/goals/goal11"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">SDG 11, Sustainable Cities and Communities</span></a><span>. At its core, the 11th goal in the SDGs challenges us to build communities where affordability, accessibility, and sustainability coexist. That looks like expanding public transportation, incentivizing the construction of affordable homes, or even planning more road diets to expand protected bike lanes.</span></span><span><span><br /><br />On paper, this week is meant to advance the UN&rsquo;s SDGs, which provide a blueprint for a sustainable tomorrow. Yet in practice, though, they serve more as a test of our collective commitment to them. Can our shared ideals stand up to the realities of action? We all want change, but how?<br /><br /></span></span><span><span>For those who don't know me well, you may be asking why I attended in the first place. Believe it or not, the parallel between global action and our locally built urban environments is closer than you think.</span></span><br /><span></span></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/20250925-160141_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Panel on “Sustainability as a Business Imperative” at UN Global Goals Week 2025 in New York City.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span><span>During UN Global Goals Week 2025, I attended </span><a href="https://luma.com/niki6y64"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">The Business Imperative for Sustainability</span></a><span>, an in-person panel featuring leaders from finance, fashion, and consumer products. Speakers discussed why sustainability is not just good for ethics but also beneficial for the private sector and business growth. Panelists explained that environmental and sustainable strategies are central to growth.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The relevance of SDG 11 to me was clear. Private entities are involved in the conversation because top-down mandates from the public sector do not always work. Buy-in from both small and large businesses toward greener initiatives will enable future communities to increase the utilization of sustainable infrastructure. This could look like electric buses, light rail, hybrid technology, and even bicycles made from recycled materials.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The private sector can help shape cities by investing in important goals and driving innovations that improve how people live and how our communities can grow.</span><span> However, lasting progress also depends on strong public-sector leadership. Communities need to make an active effort to achieve sustainable development.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>But sitting in those discussions also raised difficult questions. Who benefits when sustainability becomes a business imperative? Do these global partnerships truly reach the communities most vulnerable to climate change, or do they reinforce existing divides? If clean energy and sustainable housing remain out of reach for working families, then we are only greening inequality, not solving it. Take a city that rolls out electric scooters through a private partnership but limits them to its wealthier districts. The people who might rely on them most are effectively excluded.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>When I attended COP29 last fall in Azerbaijan, there was a lasting quote that stuck with me for global finance. I sat in on the plenary room for the second week when I heard </span><a href="https://www.fbcnews.com.fj/news/calls-for-urgent-action-at-cop29/"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">Attorney General Graham Leung of Fiji</span></a><span> deliver a passionate address, </span><span>&ldquo;On finance ambition, let me emphasize again that an ambitious outcome must focus not only on how much but also on how the finance will flow.&rdquo;</span><span> That quote is often what I think about when we have these global discussions. Oftentimes, you cannot throw money at your problems if you have no clue how it will be utilized. The same applies here for our SDGs. We all want sustainability, but sustainability for whom?</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>One of the biggest obstacles to achieving the SDGs is money, or rather, a lack thereof. The world needs </span><a href="https://unctad.org/sdg-costing/about"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">$5.4 to $6.4 trillion per year from 2023 through 2030</span></a><span> just to stay on track across key SDGs. For developing countries, that means a financing gap of about </span><a href="https://unstats.un.org/sdgs/report/2025/"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">$4 trillion annually</span></a><span> in sectors like infrastructure, health, education, water &amp; sanitation, climate mitigation/adaptation, and food security. We currently run the risk of not meeting our goals for 2030.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The gap has only widened since the pandemic. Debt burdens rise and borrowing costs increase universally. Meanwhile, global investments are growing unevenly. Some sectors, such as renewable energy and health, are seeing gains, but other areas, like water, sanitation, and food systems, struggle to attract sufficient international financing.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>This is a problem. Yet moments like Global Goals Week show the other side of the equation. The collaboration, potential, and creativity that emerge when governments, NGOs, advocates, and private actors cross paths and work together.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The beauty of Global Goals Week lies in its ambition. It creates an ecosystem of ideas and possible paths forward. But its challenge is the same as the UN&rsquo;s broader one: bridging the gap between commitment and capacity</span><span>. The idea of SDGs are not unreachable or abstract targets. It very much deals with local communities and how to help. It could look like local infrastructure investment or even more affordable homes. Without grounding the global in the local, progress risks becoming performative. All talk, no action.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Walking through New York City just a few weeks ago reminded me of how far we have come since the SDGs were adopted in 2015. They serve as the catalyst for how much further we have to go and what is left to do. We do not need bigger promises, but rather, better follow-through.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The banners and speeches matter. But the measure of success will be what happens after everyone leaves New York. Will the private sector fulfill its promises? Will we deliver on the goals, or will we need a post-2030 agenda to plan for the next generation? Only time will tell. Sustainable development will not be achieved by the loudest voices, but by the quiet persistence of local communities everywhere, turning these goals into reality.</span></span></div>  <div><div style="height: 20px; overflow: hidden; width: 100%;"></div> <hr class="styled-hr" style="width:100%;"></hr> <div style="height: 20px; overflow: hidden; width: 100%;"></div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><em>To view future readings earlier and delivered to your mailbox, please consider subscribing to my Substack. It is free, and allows you to stay up to date with my writings - you can click the button below!</em></div>  <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div> <a class="wsite-button wsite-button-small wsite-button-highlight" href="https://alancavagnaro.substack.com/" target="_blank"> <span class="wsite-button-inner">Subscribe to my substack!</span> </a> <div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div></div>

